 17592186045498 13194139535290 17592186045443 true] [17592186491428 1083 #uuid "dd23d842-a5d1-5918-948e-36db4983f82a" 13194139535290 17592186045443 true] [17592186491429 1080 "y=x - 13 - \\pi" 13194139535290 17592186045443 true] [17592186491429 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535290 17592186045443 true] [17592186491429 1083 #uuid "e306351b-f7b2-5a3a-a137-067728da4a2d" 13194139535290 17592186045443 true] [17592186491430 1080 "y=-12" 13194139535290 17592186045443 true] [17592186491430 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535290 17592186045443 true] [17592186491430 1083 #uuid "e6f5eaf7-7a98-5396-8b85-e5a1ae4f1cf7" 13194139535290 17592186045443 true] [17592186491431 1080 "y=x - 4 - \\pi" 13194139535290 17592186045443 true] [17592186491431 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535290 17592186045443 true] [17592186491431 1083 #uuid "e93d0b7a-c9db-5772-a1b5-36e791646ba3" 13194139535290 17592186045443 true] [17592186491432 1068 #edb/ref 17592186491409 13194139535290 17592186045443 true] [17592186491432 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535290 17592186045443 true] [17592186491432 1071 "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12-2x)-6{x}^{3}.$\n\n{{selection}}" 13194139535290 17592186045443 true] [17592186491432 1072 #uuid "edc669f2-8811-573d-b7ea-ebf03a0592e1" 13194139535290 17592186045443 true] [17592186491432 1074 "Let $g(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12-2x)$ and $h(x)=-6{x}^{3}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =g(x)+h(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we differentiate $g(x).$ We express $g(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12-2x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ng=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u,\\,u=12-2x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}u}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-2.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡u \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12-2x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate $h(x)=-6{x}^{3}.$ \n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}x}=-18{x}^{2}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)={g}^{′}(x)+{h}^{′}(x)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12-2x)-18{x}^{2}.\n$$" 13194139535290 17592186045443 true] [17592186491433 1080 "- 18 x^{2} - 2 \\sin{\\left(2 x - 12 \\right)}" 13194139535290 17592186045443 true] [17592186491433 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535290 17592186045443 true] [17592186491433 1083 #uuid "ef497264-895e-54be-869a-0733bc78f89e" 13194139535290 17592186045443 true] [17592186491434 1068 #edb/ref 17592186491421 13194139535290 17592186045443 true] [17592186491434 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535290 17592186045443 true] [17592186491434 1071 "Find the slope $m$ of $y =\\sin \\left(\\pi \\left(8x^{2} + 2x - 2\\right)\\right)$ at $x = 1.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535290 17592186045443 true] [17592186491434 1072 #uuid "efb4aba1-7652-55ac-8883-4dc872ffd09b" 13194139535290 17592186045443 true] [17592186491434 1074 "The slope of $y = f(x)$ at $x=1$ is given by $f'(1).$\n\nWe will calculate $f'(x)$ using the chain rule. First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n\\[\ny = \\sin{u}, \\qquad u = \\pi \\left(8x^{2} +2x - 2\\right).\n\\]\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\\[\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} = \\cos{u}, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = \\pi (16x + 2).\n\\]\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n\\begin{align*}\nf'(x) &= \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x}\\\\[5pt]\n\n&=\\cos u \\cdot \\pi (16x+2)\\\\[5pt]\n\n&=\\pi (16x+2)\\cos u\\\\[5pt]\n\n&=\\pi (16x+2)\\cos \\left(  \\pi \\left(8x^{2} +2x - 2\\right) \\right).\n\\end{align*}\n\nEvaluating at $x=1,$ we find\n\n\\begin{align}\nf'(1) & =\\pi (16\\cdot1+2)\\cos \\left(  \\pi \\left(8\\cdot1^{2} +2\\cdot1 - 2\\right) \\right) \\\\[3pt]\n\n& =18\\pi \\cos(8\\pi) \\\\[3pt]\n&= 18\\pi \\cdot (1) \\\\[3pt]\n&= 18 \\pi\n\\end{align}\n\nTherefore, the slope $m$ of $y = f(x)$ at $x = 1$ is \n\\[\nm = f'(1) = \\bbox[4pt, border: 1px solid lightgray]{18 \\pi}.\n\\]" 13194139535290 17592186045443 true] [17592186491435 1080 "y=- 3 x + 3 \\pi" 13194139535290 17592186045443 true] [17592186491435 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535290 17592186045443 true] [17592186491435 1083 #uuid "f4e4f64c-13d8-53d9-a08b-47cebaf795b4" 13194139535290 17592186045443 true] [17592186491436 1068 #edb/ref 17592186491418 13194139535290 17592186045443 true] [17592186491436 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535290 17592186045443 true] [17592186491436 1071 "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $y=11\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-1)$ at the point $(1+\\pi ,-11).$\n\n{{selection}}" 13194139535290 17592186045443 true] [17592186491436 1072 #uuid "f70d4be3-a4f6-5618-b5df-d3aca4e557dc" 13194139535290 17592186045443 true] [17592186491436 1074 "First, the slope of $y=f(x)$ at $x=1+\\pi $ is given by ${f}^{′}(1+\\pi ).$\n\nWe will calculate ${f}^{′}(x)$ using the chain rule. First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=11\\operatorname{cos}⁡u,\\,u=x-1.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=-11\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =-11\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-11\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=1+\\pi ,$ we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}m={f}^{′}(1+\\pi ) & =-11\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1+\\pi -1) \\\\  & =-11\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the equation of the tangent at the point $(1+\\pi ,-11):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(-11) & =0(x-(1+\\pi )) \\\\ y+11 & =0 \\\\ y & =-11.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535290 17592186045443